+## 2026-09-04 16:xxZ — REQ-CONDUCTOR-VERDICT-4 is live and its real test has NOT happened
+
+The spec table marks it Implemented with tests, and the tests are real (6, mutation-proven). A
+reader would reasonably conclude it is proven. It is not, and the distinction matters enough to
+write down.
+
+**What it fixes.** Two capstones died this week declaring `verdict_class: partial` over milestones
+whose upstreams had been cascade-blocked — v605 and v608. `partial` is the one enum member the
+conductor retries, so each burned three attempts on three identical artifacts and was retired. The
+prompt now tells task authors that external incompleteness is `blocked`, which is terminal.
+
+**Why the one capstone since does not test it.** V611's capstone (exp6983) survived, writing
+`verdict_class: null` with `complete_null_v611_capstone_no_non_circular_science_positive`. But V611
+was not cascade-blocked — the capstone had a genuine null result, and `null` was the correct class
+whether or not the prompt said anything. The partial-versus-blocked decision never arose.
+
+**So the fix is shipped, live, and unexercised.** The test case is the next capstone over a
+milestone whose upstream tasks were retired or gate-blocked. When that happens, the thing to check
+is whether it declares `blocked` and is accepted on first write, or declares `partial` and burns
+three attempts as v605 and v608 did.
+
+Recorded because this is the same shape as the `arc-news-watch` timeout fix, where the verification
+run finished inside the OLD budget and therefore proved nothing about the new one. That distinction
+was written down; this one nearly was not, on the grounds that "nothing happened" is not a finding.
+A fix whose test has not occurred is not nothing — it is an open question wearing a closed
+requirement's clothes.
